1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:03,483
How does water reach the top of a tree?

2
00:00:03,483 --> 00:00:05,931
Xylem, root pressure and transpiration pull.

3
00:00:06,527 --> 00:00:10,927
Look at this eucalyptus tree, and a person standing

4
00:00:10,927 --> 00:00:14,491
beside it. Now let us climb. Twenty metres, sixty

5
00:00:14,491 --> 00:00:19,094
metres, one hundred metres, and finally, about one hundred

6
00:00:19,094 --> 00:00:22,693
and eighty metres. Far below, in the soil near

7
00:00:22,693 --> 00:00:26,207
a root, is one tiny drop of water. So

8
00:00:26,207 --> 00:00:29,158
how can this drop get all the way up there?

9
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:32,183
The first gate is the root hair. A root

10
00:00:32,183 --> 00:00:35,920
has many fine, microscopic projections, and each one is

11
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:38,827
a root hair. Water sits in thin films between

12
00:00:38,827 --> 00:00:42,653
the soil particles. Our drop moves towards a root

13
00:00:42,653 --> 00:00:46,020
hair and crosses into it. This is where water

14
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:49,212
enters the plant. Each root hair is tiny, but

15
00:00:49,212 --> 00:00:52,794
together they make a huge absorbing region. So how

16
00:00:52,794 --> 00:00:55,791
does water move in? The answer is osmosis.

17
00:00:56,393 --> 00:00:59,196
Now follow the drop inside the root. The soil

18
00:00:59,196 --> 00:01:01,763
water is more dilute, and the cell sap of

19
00:01:01,763 --> 00:01:05,039
the root hair is more concentrated. So water moves

20
00:01:05,039 --> 00:01:08,253
into the root hair by osmosis. The same drop

21
00:01:08,253 --> 00:01:12,373
then passes from cell to cell, through the neighbouring

22
00:01:12,373 --> 00:01:16,733
root cells. Finally, it enters a large vertical tube:

23
00:01:16,733 --> 00:01:19,973
the xylem vessel. Many root hairs feed many xylem

24
00:01:19,973 --> 00:01:24,464
pathways. So the path is: soil, root hair, root cells, xylem.

25
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:27,705
Many root hairs keep adding water, and it collects

26
00:01:27,705 --> 00:01:30,800
in the xylem. Pressure builds up there, and it

27
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,783
can push the water upward. This is called root

28
00:01:33,783 --> 00:01:38,840
pressure. You can see it in a simple activity.

29
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,470
Cut a stem, and fix a glass tube to

30
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:44,420
it. The water level rises from M one to

31
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:47,280
M two. But notice the arrow. It is only

32
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:48,931
a small push, and it is not the main force.

33
00:01:49,587 --> 00:01:53,324
Now picture the whole tree. The xylem runs unbroken

34
00:01:53,324 --> 00:01:56,953
from the roots to the crown. Root pressure pushes

35
00:01:56,953 --> 00:01:59,879
from the bottom, but next to the height of

36
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:02,817
the tree, it is small. In fact, root pressure

37
00:02:02,817 --> 00:02:05,977
is seldom high, and sometimes it may be nil.

38
00:02:05,977 --> 00:02:08,727
Then what lifts the water to the top? To

39
00:02:08,727 --> 00:02:10,918
find out, go up to a leaf.

40
00:02:11,507 --> 00:02:13,970
Inside a leaf, the xylem ends near the mesophyll

41
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:16,716
cells. Water reaches the mesophyll region. From the moist

42
00:02:16,716 --> 00:02:20,722
surfaces of these cells, water evaporates into the air

43
00:02:20,722 --> 00:02:24,010
spaces as vapour. The vapour moves out through the

44
00:02:24,010 --> 00:02:28,027
stomata into the atmosphere. This loss of water by

45
00:02:28,027 --> 00:02:33,092
evaporation from the leaf is called transpiration. Molecule after

46
00:02:33,092 --> 00:02:36,367
molecule leaves in this way. A little water also

47
00:02:36,367 --> 00:02:39,858
evaporates through the lenticels of the stem. So what

48
00:02:39,858 --> 00:02:41,478
happens to the water below?

49
00:02:42,137 --> 00:02:44,147
Think of the xylem as a tall, narrow tube,

50
00:02:44,147 --> 00:02:49,417
completely filled with a continuous column of water. At

51
00:02:49,417 --> 00:02:52,477
the top, one water molecule evaporates from the leaf.

52
00:02:52,477 --> 00:02:55,407
The molecule below moves up to take its place,

53
00:02:55,407 --> 00:02:58,427
then the next, then the next. The movement passes

54
00:02:58,427 --> 00:03:00,889
down the whole column, and the column moves as

55
00:03:00,889 --> 00:03:05,697
one connected stream. This pulling effect is the transpiration

56
00:03:05,697 --> 00:03:07,835
pull. At the bottom, root pressure is a small

57
00:03:07,835 --> 00:03:11,950
push. At the top, transpiration is the main pull.

58
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:14,351
So the main pull comes from above, and the

59
00:03:14,351 --> 00:03:16,468
push from below is minor and variable.

60
00:03:17,277 --> 00:03:20,571
Why does the water column not break? Because water

61
00:03:20,571 --> 00:03:24,009
molecules attract one another. So when the top molecule

62
00:03:24,009 --> 00:03:27,977
is pulled, its neighbours follow. The column stretches a

63
00:03:27,977 --> 00:03:30,461
little, but it stays connected. It is like drinking

64
00:03:30,461 --> 00:03:32,737
through a straw: pull at the top, and the

65
00:03:32,737 --> 00:03:35,737
whole column of water rises. In the same way,

66
00:03:35,737 --> 00:03:38,637
one continuous pathway runs from the root, through the

67
00:03:38,637 --> 00:03:39,908
stem, to the leaf.

68
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:43,810
Your turn. Build the water pathway. A: where does

69
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:47,512
water enter the plant? B: which process helps water

70
00:03:47,512 --> 00:03:50,270
enter and move towards the xylem? C: which tissue

71
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:54,185
carries water upward? D: in a tall tree, which

72
00:03:54,185 --> 00:03:57,630
is the main pull, root pressure or transpiration? E:

73
00:03:57,630 --> 00:04:01,390
put these in order: xylem, soil, leaf, root hair,

74
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:05,970
atmosphere. Take your time, and pause the video if

75
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:07,101
you need to.

76
00:04:18,510 --> 00:04:22,710
Let us check. A: the root hair. B: osmosis.

77
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:28,200
C: the xylem. D: transpiration pull. E: soil, root

78
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:34,030
hair, xylem, leaf, atmosphere. Roots push a little, but

79
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:35,541
leaves pull the most.

80
00:04:36,383 --> 00:04:39,123
Follow one last drop. It enters a root hair,

81
00:04:39,123 --> 00:04:41,976
and moves from cell to cell by osmosis. It

82
00:04:41,976 --> 00:04:45,883
enters the xylem, where a small, variable push from

83
00:04:45,883 --> 00:04:50,163
root pressure helps from below. At the leaf, water

84
00:04:50,163 --> 00:04:54,179
evaporates. This is transpiration, and it pulls the whole

85
00:04:54,179 --> 00:04:58,517
water column upward. This is the transpiration pull, the

86
00:04:58,517 --> 00:05:02,340
main pull. Finally, the water vapour leaves into the

87
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:06,456
atmosphere. This continuous moving column is the transpiration stream.

88
00:05:06,456 --> 00:05:11,623
So: roots absorb, xylem connects, leaves transpire, and the

89
00:05:11,623 --> 00:05:14,377
transpiration pull lifts the water. Thank you for watching,

90
00:05:14,377 --> 00:05:18,174
and see you in the next lesson.
